@@s001 Oral glossary
@@s002 ora:action now,dato:returned value,no:the problem,ok:result
@@s003 Oral glossary
@@s004 The exercises ask to explain "using the concepts and vocabulary introduced during the lecture". The oral does too. Here every word has the definition as the course uses it (in English, to say it that way) and one line in plain English to understand it.
@@s005 Lecture 1: concurrency and mutual exclusion
@@s006 Term
@@s007 As the course says it
@@s008 In plain words
@@s009 Page
@@s010 <b>Thread</b>
@@s011 an independent sequence of program statements; each thread has its own local memory and can communicate via shared memory
@@s012 a "thread" of instructions that goes on by itself
@@s013 <a href="l01-thread.html">Thread</a>
@@s014 <b>Concurrent</b>
@@s015 computation may be split in arbitrary blocks; multiple streams can run in one core
@@s016 many things going on in the same period, even on a single core
@@s017 <a href="l01-perche-concorrenza.html">Why</a>
@@s018 <b>Atomic</b>
@@s019 executed as if it is a single (indivisible) operation
@@s020 no other thread can "slip in" in the middle
@@s021 <a href="l01-interleaving.html">Interleaving</a>
@@s022 <b>Interleaving</b>
@@s023 a possible sequence of operations for a concurrent program (not for a thread)
@@s024 a possible order in which the steps of all the threads are mixed
@@s025 <a href="l01-interleaving.html">Interleaving</a>
@@s026 <b>Race condition</b>
@@s027 the result of the computation depends on the interleavings of the operations
@@s028 the result changes depending on who goes first
@@s029 <a href="l01-interleaving.html">Interleaving</a>
@@s030 <b>Data race</b> (L1)
@@s031 two concurrent threads access a shared memory location and at least one access is a write
@@s032 two threads touch the same variable and one writes
@@s033 <a href="l01-interleaving.html">Interleaving</a>
@@s034 <b>Critical section</b>
@@s035 a part of the program where concurrent access must be restricted
@@s036 the piece of code that only one thread at a time must execute
@@s037 <a href="l01-lock.html">Lock</a>
@@s038 <b>Mutual exclusion</b>
@@s039 two or more threads cannot execute their critical section at the same time
@@s040 in the critical section there is one thread at a time
@@s041 <a href="l01-lock.html">Lock</a>
@@s042 <b>Deadlock</b> (absence of)
@@s043 threads eventually exit the critical section allowing other threads to enter
@@s044 nobody stays blocked for ever
@@s045 <a href="l01-lock.html">Lock</a>
@@s046 <b>Starvation</b> (absence of)
@@s047 if a thread is ready to enter the critical section, it must eventually do so
@@s048 whoever waits sooner or later gets in
@@s049 <a href="l02-fairness.html">Fairness</a>
@@s050 <b>Reentrant lock</b>
@@s051 a lock that can be locked more than once by the same thread
@@s052 if you already have the lock, taking it again does not block you
@@s053 <a href="l01-lock.html">Lock</a>
@@s054 Lecture 2: shared memory I
@@s055 Term
@@s056 As the course says it
@@s057 In plain words
@@s058 Page
@@s059 <b>Monitor</b>
@@s060 a shared state and meaningful operations on the state that protect it from corruption, even when performed concurrently
@@s061 a class with private data, all methods in mutual exclusion and wait queues
@@s062 <a href="l02-monitor.html">Monitor</a>
@@s063 <b>Condition variable</b>
@@s064 a queue where the monitor can put threads to wait (<code>await</code>, <code>signal</code>, <code>signalAll</code>)
@@s065 a waiting room for whoever must wait for something to happen
@@s066 <a href="l02-monitor.html">Monitor</a>
@@s067 <b>Fairness</b>
@@s068 in this course: absence of starvation
@@s069 nobody waits for ever
@@s070 <a href="l02-fairness.html">Fairness</a>
@@s071 <b>Mesa semantics</b>
@@s072 signalled threads go back to the entry queue and compete again (Java)
@@s073 whoever is woken up must get back in line
@@s074 <a href="l02-fairness.html">Fairness</a>
@@s075 <b>Busy-wait</b>
@@s076 a thread repeatedly checks a condition instead of blocking
@@s077 checking in a loop instead of sleeping: forbidden in the exercises
@@s078 <a href="l02-fairness.html">Fairness</a>
@@s079 <b>Intrinsic lock</b>
@@s080 the lock (monitor) every Java object has, used via <code>synchronized</code>
@@s081 the lock that every object already has inside
@@s082 <a href="l02-synchronized.html">synchronized</a>
@@s083 <b>Visibility</b>
@@s084 without synchronization the CPU may keep data in registers/cache not visible to threads on other CPUs
@@s085 a write that another thread might never see
@@s086 <a href="l02-visibility.html">Visibility</a>
@@s087 <b>Reordering</b>
@@s088 without data dependencies or synchronization, CPU or JIT may reorder operations
@@s089 instructions swapped around to go faster
@@s090 <a href="l02-visibility.html">Visibility</a>
@@s091 <b>volatile</b>
@@s092 a weak form of synchronization: ensures visibility, prevents reordering, no mutual exclusion
@@s093 "always read the updated value", but it does not protect <code>x++</code>
@@s094 <a href="l02-visibility.html">Visibility</a>
@@s095 Lecture 3: Java Memory Model
@@s096 Term
@@s097 As the course says it
@@s098 In plain words
@@s099 Page
@@s100 <b>Sequential consistency</b>
@@s101 preserves intra-thread program order and operations appear to occur at once
@@s102 the "normal" behaviour you expect
@@s103 <a href="l03-azioni-esecuzioni.html">Actions</a>
@@s104 <b>Action</b>
@@s105 a pair t(o): thread identifier and operation; variable access, synchronization, or other
@@s106 "who does what", for example <code>t1(2)</code>
@@s107 <a href="l03-azioni-esecuzioni.html">Actions</a>
@@s108 <b>Execution</b>
@@s109 a sequence of actions
@@s110 an interleaving written as actions
@@s111 <a href="l03-azioni-esecuzioni.html">Actions</a>
@@s112 <b>Program order</b>
@@s113 the intra-thread order: a before b if a would be executed before b when the thread runs sequentially
@@s114 the order of the lines inside a thread
@@s115 <a href="l03-azioni-esecuzioni.html">Actions</a>
@@s116 <b>Happens-before</b>
@@s117 a partial order a -> b defined by the rules (program order, monitor, volatile, init, start, termination, transitive)
@@s118 "a certainly comes before b, and b sees the effect of a"
@@s119 <a href="l03-happens-before.html">Happens-before</a>
@@s120 <b>Synchronization order</b>
@@s121 a total order of the synchronization actions of an execution, consistent with mutual exclusion and happens-before
@@s122 the order in which lock, unlock, start, join… happen
@@s123 <a href="l03-data-race.html">Data race</a>
@@s124 <b>Conflicting actions</b>
@@s125 accesses on the same non-volatile variable, at least one is a write
@@s126 the pairs of accesses that are potentially dangerous
@@s127 <a href="l03-data-race.html">Data race</a>
@@s128 <b>Data race</b> (JMM)
@@s129 two actions are conflicting and not ordered by happens-before
@@s130 dangerous accesses without a guaranteed order
@@s131 <a href="l03-data-race.html">Data race</a>
@@s132 <b>Correctly synchronized</b>
@@s133 none of the executions contains data races
@@s134 a correct program according to the JMM
@@s135 <a href="l03-data-race.html">Data race</a>
@@s136 Lecture 4: shared memory II
@@s137 Term
@@s138 As the course says it
@@s139 In plain words
@@s140 Page
@@s141 <b>Thread-safe class</b>
@@s142 no concurrent execution of method calls or field accesses on objects of the class results in data races on the fields of the class
@@s143 used by several threads, its fields never have data races
@@s144 <a href="l04-thread-safety.html">Thread-safety</a>
@@s145 <b>Thread-safe program</b>
@@s146 a concurrent program is thread-safe iff it is data race free
@@s147 no data race in the whole program
@@s148 <a href="l04-thread-safety.html">Thread-safety</a>
@@s149 <b>Escaping</b>
@@s150 class state can be accessed other than through its (synchronized) methods
@@s151 a field that "escapes" from the protection of the locks
@@s152 <a href="l04-thread-safety.html">Thread-safety</a>
@@s153 <b>Safe publication</b>
@@s154 after initialization, the initial values of the fields are visible to any thread accessing the object
@@s155 whoever receives the object sees it already properly built
@@s156 <a href="l04-pubblicazione.html">Publication</a>
@@s157 <b>Immutable field</b>
@@s158 its value does not change after initialization
@@s159 a constant: no need to protect it after construction
@@s160 <a href="l04-pubblicazione.html">Publication</a>
@@s161 <b>Semaphore</b>
@@s162 allows up to c threads in the critical section (<code>acquire</code>, <code>release</code>)
@@s163 a bouncer with c places
@@s164 <a href="l04-semafori.html">Semaphores</a>
@@s165 <b>Barrier</b>
@@s166 waits until a number of parties reach a point in their computation
@@s167 one starts only when everybody has arrived
@@s168 <a href="l04-semafori.html">Semaphores</a>
@@s169 <b>Instance confinement</b>
@@s170 encapsulating a thread-unsafe object into a thread-safe class
@@s171 closing a fragile object inside a protected class
@@s172 <a href="l04-confinamento.html">Confinement</a>
@@s173 <b>Compound action</b>
@@s174 multiple method calls or field accesses that must happen within one critical section (check-then-act)
@@s175 "check and then act", all done under the same lock
@@s176 <a href="l04-confinamento.html">Confinement</a>
@@s177 The two most common confusions
@@s178 Race condition ≠ data race
@@s179 A race condition concerns the <b>result</b>; a data race concerns the <b>accesses</b>. You can have one without the other: <code>addIfAbsent</code> on a synchronized list has a race condition but no data race (<a href="l04-confinamento.html">L4</a>).
@@s180 Thread-safe ≠ "seems to work"
@@s181 In the course thread-safe has a precise definition: absence of data races on the fields. A test that finds no errors <b>does not</b> prove thread-safety (exercise 4.2.4).
@@s182 Race condition without data race, and vice versa
